WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.960
- Thank you to Surfshark VPN
for supporting this PBS video.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.560
Picture a small town on the
coast of 19th century Wales.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.360
A family gathers around the hearth,

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.270
a pan of butter toffee
bubbling on the embers.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:15.390
The house is decorated with
branches of fragrant pine,

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.210
and the glow from small
candles perched on the limbs

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.520
of a Christmas tree lights the room.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.280
Through a window, they see
torch light coming up the path

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.010
towards the cottage,
accompanied by chanting.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.335
Suddenly three hard raps at the door.

00:00:28.335 --> 00:00:29.760
(door knocking)
(mysterious music)

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.980
It flies open and an impossibly tall

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.860
and terrifying figure
looms over the family.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.250
draped in dirty white linens,
its head is a horse's skull

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.830
with glass baubles in the eye sockets.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:43.200
Ribbons tied about the skull are streaming

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.790
in the wind behind it like the hair

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.710
of some horrific sea monster.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.560
Then all at once the
group begins a merry tune,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.150
stomping and clapping in time

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.670
with the grim horse's head bobbing along.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.130
The family responds with a
joyful verse of their own,

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.920
and before long, the whole
gaggle has made their way

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.830
into the cottage and are
clanking tankards together.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:03.990
Sounds like we just stepped into a scene

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:05.310
from A Nightmare Before Christmas,

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.680
when in fact it is a
centuries-old tradition

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.110
from Wales called Mari Lwyd.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.090
One, it seems, of many to blend a bit

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:14.531
of scary into their Merry Christmas.

00:01:14.531 --> 00:01:16.320
(proud music)

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.220
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka, and this is Monstrum.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.230
Mari Lwyd, though often used as a name

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.180
to describe the monster is actually

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:25.980
the name of the tradition itself.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.070
This Christmastime creature is
composed of an animal skull,

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.860
usually equine or bovine
attached to the top

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.590
of a pole with a sackcloth
or sheet draped down

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.960
from the head to obscure
the bearer beneath.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.690
The skull is often elaborately decorated.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.540
Some accounts detail glass
ornaments as eyes along

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.210
with ribbons, bells,
and other accoutrements.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.250
One thing is always present, however,

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.280
and that is the group that
travels with Mari Lwyd.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.680
To help us gain a better
understanding of this tradition,

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.440
it is important to first
discuss an ongoing practice

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.810
in the British Isles called wassailing.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.180
Wassailing is a Christmastime
tradition dating back

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:01.590
to the Middle Ages.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.420
Although the specifics
of the practice vary

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.210
from region to region, it
generally involves a group

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.090
of people traveling house
to house, sharing drinks

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.040
and singing with the village folk.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.110
The tradition usually involves some form

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:14.580
of performative bartering,

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.260
like taking a drink from the wassail bowl

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.600
in exchange for some type of gift.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.120
Although wassailing is still
practiced in some pockets

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.490
of the British Isles,
the tradition has grown

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.190
and evolved over time into
what many people today

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:27.660
would call caroling.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.240
So how does a skull fit into all of this?

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.190
We'll get into the varied
opinions on the origins

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.250
a bit later, but for now,
let's talk logistics.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.290
The Mari Lwyd is one of the main members

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.480
of the Welsh wassailing entourage.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.150
Along with our skeletal friend,
there is usually someone

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.740
who leads the group on their rounds

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.660
and is the most formally dressed.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.520
Other characters like the famous violent,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.120
rosy-cheeked puppets Punch and
Judy are oft included as well

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.980
This troop makes their
way through the town,

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.800
and upon arriving at a home
will begin singing a call

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.650
and response verse of sorts
with the tenants within.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.780
There are many versions
of the song, both old

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.600
and contemporary, but
generally the song consists

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.370
of the wassailers asking for permission

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.830
to enter the home to feast and drink.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.230
The people within then
respond in song alike

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.900
with excuses for why
they can't let them in.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.150
This carries on until the
people inside can produce

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:17.280
no further excuses, and are required

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.430
to grant entry, mass merriment ensues.

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:23.461
Again, the specifics vary by
region and by time period,

00:03:23.461 --> 00:03:24.294
(child screaming)

00:03:24.294 --> 00:03:26.160
but generally once
inside the band partakes

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.020
in holiday treats and share ale

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.520
while their cranial creation would

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.140
playfully chase after the children

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:34.410
and adults eliciting
screams and laughter alike.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.170
Some elaborate Mari Lwyd
creations might even feature

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.180
a snapping mandible made possible

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.850
through a lever or pole
string contraption.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.040
Interestingly enough, this is actually one

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.010
of the British Isle's many
hooded animal traditions.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.840
There are the Old Horse, Old Ball,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.090
and Old Tup traditions
of Northern England,

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.970
the Hoodening of Kent, and
the Broad of the Cotswolds.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:56.220
Although these others
often used a much less

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.690
menacing hobby horse of sorts,

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:00.240
and aren't always
associated with Christmas.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.880
So where exactly did
this tradition come from,

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.640
and what does the name
Mari Lwyd actually mean?

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.040
There is some disagreement on both counts.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.410
Iorwerth Peate, a 20th century Welsh poet

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.330
and scholar wrote that he believed

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.400
the Mari Lwyd to be a pagan tradition

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.770
that predates Christianity much like many

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:19.440
of the other traditions
practiced around the holidays.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.350
However, there is no historical evidence

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.930
that really supports this assumption.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.090
Nevertheless, he and others latched

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.910
onto the supposed ancient
origins of the custom.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:32.340
Peate believed the practice
was rooted in fear.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.790
The winter holidays mark a time
of darkness, cold and death.

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:39.000
A skeletal horse skull does
certainly evoke demise.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.500
Although Peate originally thought

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.930
that Mary must be a
reference to the Virgin Mary,

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.700
he later backtracked and claimed

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.590
that the word is most likely a version

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:49.200
of the word "mare", as in female horse.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.150
Other Welsh scholars of
the 20th century seem

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:53.790
to agree with him, for the most part,

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:55.800
Ellen Ettlinger, a British art collector

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.530
and folklorist believed Mari
Lwyd to have connections

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.620
to the Pagan holiday,
Samhain, evoking death.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.990
Some folklorists suggest
that it may at one point have

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.450
symbolized fertility, and over time simply

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.280
became associated with good luck.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.250
There are many equine
divinities in the Celtic Isles,

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:15.300
like Rhiannon in Wales, Epona
in Gaul, and Macha in Ireland.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.130
But there is very little
evidence to support

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.230
these theories given
the lack of references

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.180
to these practices in any documents

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.950
from the late medieval era.

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:26.229
There is however plenty of
documentation from the 16th

00:05:26.229 --> 00:05:29.730
and 17th centuries for hobby
horses throughout the region.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.820
Costume folklore characters
meant to evoke real horses,

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:34.650
they were often used by jesters

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.260
and other merrymakers
during the Middle Ages.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:38.910
We see references to them as early

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:41.880
as the 1300s in a poem by a Welsh author

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:44.310
where he poked some fun at the hobby horse

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.800
as having a miserable pair of lathe legs,

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.530
AKA, really skinny wood legs.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:51.900
One of the earliest
known written references

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:55.620
we have to the Mari Lwyd
appears only in the 19th century

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.230
where it is condemned as immoral.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.930
Rather than being part of
some ancient pagan tradition,

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:04.590
the Mari Lwyd is likely a
uniquely menacing Welsh adaptation

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:07.800
of the British Isles' many
hooded animal traditions.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.650
The etymology of the Mari
Lwyd is thus unsurprisingly

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:13.200
similarly murky with many scholars divided

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.970
on the actual origins.

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:16.140
It has become consensus

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:18.120
that the term likely means gray mare,

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:19.890
particularly since the word "llwyd"

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:21.840
translates to gray in Welsh.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.010
This would also make sense

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:24.990
as it parallels other
hobby horse traditions

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:28.410
throughout the region, namely
in Ireland and the Isle of Man

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:30.360
In the Celtic language Manx, it is known

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:34.050
as the "laare vane", which
translates to white mare.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:36.030
But a connection to the
Virgin Mary still exists

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.730
in many modern iterations
of the Mari Lwyd,

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:41.190
with very clear references
to the mother of Jesus

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:43.080
in several of the traditional songs.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.360
But hey, it is Yule time after all.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.250
Despite this inclusion
of Christian elements

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:49.740
in the tradition, the
Mari Lwyd was not a part

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.360
of the public consciousness for most

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.910
of the early 20th century
because of disapproval

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:57.000
from local Christian clergy
and general movement away

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.420
from more ancient practices in
an increasingly modern world.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:03.210
However, by the mid-century,
Mari Lwyd started creeping

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:04.950
its way back into the light.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:07.807
A 1941 poem by Vernon Watkins called

00:07:07.807 --> 00:07:10.470
"The Ballad of the Mari
Lwyd" was well-received

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:14.347
in Welsh literary circles, as
was Susan Cooper's 1977 novel

00:07:14.347 --> 00:07:17.370
"Silver on the Tree", in
which Mari Lwyd appears

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:20.790
as an undead unicorn and
protector of a magic sword

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:23.880
in the mythical sunken lands
off the coast of Wales.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.580
How that's not a movie
yet is a mystery to me.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.890
Later, the Mari Lwyd
became better known outside

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.020
of Wales via the tabletop
role-playing game,

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:32.520
Dungeons and Dragons.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.950
In D&amp;D, the Mari Lwyd is an undead horse

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.500
that speaks only in rhyme
with a chattering jaw,

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.440
and seeks entry into people's
homes where it devours food

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.540
and drink before needing a place to sleep,

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.590
behavior clearly inspired
by the original folklore.

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:48.960
In 2000, the artist Clive
Hicks-Jenkins featured Mari Lwyd

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.660
in a series of drawings
that personified the monster

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.510
as the horrific bringer
of his father's death.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:57.870
This series was accompanied
by poems by Catriona Urquhart,

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.790
titled "The Mayor's Tale".

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:02.700
Local Celebrations that Included
the Mari Lwyd became more

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:04.350
and more commonplace throughout Wales

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:07.320
as the 20th century wore
on, culminating in a turn

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.210
of the millennium jubilee in the town

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:12.300
of Aberystwyth wherein they
rang in the new century

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:14.580
with an elaborate
procession throughout town.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:17.340
Their top-billed attraction
being a massive representation

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:21.060
of the creature dubbed "The
World's Largest Mari Lwyd".

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.520
Christmastime celebrations
that include the tall, dark,

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.100
and fearsome creation
continue throughout Wales

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:28.440
and parts of England to this day.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.120
as regular Monstrum viewers may know,

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:32.580
Christmastime monsters
lurk in the holiday shadows

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:34.800
of many cultures and traditions.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:36.840
There's Frau Perchta,
also known regionally

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:39.810
as the Italian La Befana,
the Russian Babushka,

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:41.640
or the Slavic Baba Yaga.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.080
From Iceland, we have Gryla

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.300
and her offspring the prankster Yule Lads,

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.720
not to mention our favorite
murder-floof, the Yule cat.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.810
And of course, most famous,
Krampus, the half-goat,

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:55.290
half-demon anti-Santa who
punishes naughty children.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:57.930
And this begs the question,
why during a holiday

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:00.120
so associated with joy and festivity

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:04.020
do we have so many monsters,
so much darkness and fear?

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.360
To answer that we need
to look at celebrations

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:07.860
of the winter solstice.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:09.720
The winter solstice has always been

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.310
viewed as an important time.

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:13.380
The harvest seasons have
now come to a close.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:15.330
The cold chill begins to blow in

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:18.750
and weather becomes
increasingly less hospitable.

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:20.850
Traditionally without
much work to do for those

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:22.890
who would otherwise be plowing the fields,

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:24.840
they often pass the hours by drinking

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.730
and telling stories by the fire.

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:29.460
The Roman festival of
Saturnalia occurring on

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:33.060
or around the solstice was
a holiday of opposites.

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:34.830
Masters would serve their slaves,

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:36.720
the rich would garner gifts for the poor,

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:39.030
and enslaved and free men alike would take

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:40.950
to the streets in disguise to engage

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:43.470
in all manner of Roman debauchery.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:45.600
Yule was a Nordic and Germanic tradition,

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:47.910
which involved feasting and celebration,

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:50.220
but was also rife with animal sacrifice

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:53.520
and the ceremonial smearing
of blood all over the temple,

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:55.380
and the worshipers in attendance.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.780
Humans have always had a
complicated relationship

00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.250
with the coming of winter.

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:02.460
The cold, dark depths of our
chilliest season bring about

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:05.160
a sense of wonder and also dread.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:06.720
Whether we choose to approach it head on

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.210
via the spooky Christmas
characters of Europe

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:12.030
or to sugarcoat it with
candy canes and gum drops.

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:14.760
Winter is a time of
contemplative reflection

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:17.580
on the small death of
our world as it freezes.

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:19.650
and sometimes the merry and the scary

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:21.060
are a perfect marriage.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:23.130
As is the case with the Mari Lwyd,

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:25.830
which despite its grim
visage is actually viewed

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:28.560
by all participants as jolly good fun.

